<article class="env-remark" id="0BA7">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0BA7">Remark <span data-tag="0BA7">72.10.6</span>.</a> Let $k$ be a finite field. Let $K/k$ be a geometrically irreducible field extension. Then $K$ is the limit of geometrically irreducible finite type $k$-algebras $A$. Given $A$ the estimates of Lang and Weil <span class="cite">[<a href="/bibliography/LW">LW</a>]</span>, show that for $n \gg 0$ there exists an $k$-algebra homomorphism $A \to k'$ with $k'/k$ of degree $n$. Analyzing the argument given in the proof of Lemma <a href="/tag/0B85" data-tag="0B85">72.10.5</a> we see that if $X$ is a quasi-separated algebraic space over $k$ and $X_ K$ is a scheme, then $X$ is a scheme. If we ever need this result we will precisely formulate it and prove it here. </p>
</article>